## Proof

**Setup and reduction to a single inequality.**

Let $ON$ be horizontal and $OM$ make an angle $\alpha$ with $ON$ (the inclined plane). Gravity points perpendicular to $ON$, i.e. vertically. Place a triangle with side $\overline{AB}$ parallel to $OM$. Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$, $|AB|=2c$, and let $\hat d=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha)$ be the unit vector along $AB$, $\hat n=(-\sin\alpha,\cos\alpha)$ the unit normal pointing above the plane. Write the apex as
$$C=M+u\,\hat d+v\,\hat n,\qquad v>0.$$
The reflection of $C$ across the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ (the line through $M$ in direction $\hat n$) is
$$C'=M-u\,\hat d+v\,\hat n.$$
The centroids are
$$G=M+\tfrac{u}{3}\hat d+\tfrac{v}{3}\hat n,\qquad G'=M-\tfrac{u}{3}\hat d+\tfrac{v}{3}\hat n.$$

A vertical line (perpendicular to $ON$) through $G$ meets the line $AB$ (parametrized as $M+t\hat d$) at the parameter $t$ satisfying $t\cos\alpha=\tfrac{u\cos\alpha-v\sin\alpha}{3}$, i.e.
$$t=\frac{u-v\tan\alpha}{3}.$$
Requiring this point to lie on the segment $\overline{AB}$ gives $|u-v\tan\alpha|\le 3c$. Likewise the line through $G'$ gives $|u+v\tan\alpha|\le 3c$. Since $v\tan\alpha\ge 0$, the two inequalities together are equivalent to the single condition
$$\boxed{|u|+v\tan\alpha\le 3c.}\tag{$\ast$}$$

**Reformulation per side.** For a triangle with area $K$ and sides $a,b,c_{0}$ (where $c_{0}$ is chosen as the base), we have $c=c_{0}/2$, $v=h=2K/c_{0}$ (the altitude), and $|u|=d=|b^{2}-a^{2}|/(2c_{0})$ (the distance from the foot of the altitude to the midpoint of the base). Substituting into $(\ast)$ and solving for $\tan\alpha$ yields the admissible value
$$R_{c_{0}}=\frac{3c_{0}^{2}-|a^{2}-b^{2}|}{4K}.$$
The triangle is stable on the inclined plane at angle $\alpha$ iff **some** side $c_{0}$ satisfies $\tan\alpha\le R_{c_{0}}$, i.e. iff $\tan\alpha\le \max_{c_{0}\in\{a,b,c\}}R_{c_{0}}$. Hence the desired supremum of $\alpha$ equals
$$\arctan\!\Big(\inf_{T}\;\max_{c_{0}} R_{c_{0}}\Big).$$

**The maximum is attained at the longest side.** Let $r\ge q\ge p$ be the sides. Then
$$R_{r}-R_{p}=\frac{4r^{2}-3p^{2}-q^{2}-|p^{2}-q^{2}|}{4K}\ge 0,$$
because if $p\ge q$ this equals $(r^{2}-p^{2})/K\ge 0$, and if $q\ge p$ it equals $(2r^{2}-p^{2}-q^{2})/(2K)\ge 0$ since $p^{2}+q^{2}\le 2r^{2}$. By symmetry $R_{r}\ge R_{q}$ as well. Thus $\max R_{c_{0}}=R_{r}$, the value at the longest side.

**Minimizing $R_{r}$ over all triangles.** Normalize the longest side to $r=1$ and place it on the $x$-axis from $(0,0)$ to $(1,0)$, with the apex at $(x,y)$, $y>0$. The condition that this side is longest is $x^{2}+y^{2}\le 1$ and $(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}\le 1$. The area gives $4K=2y$, and
$$|s_{1}^{2}-s_{2}^{2}|=|(x^{2}+y^{2})-((x-1)^{2}+y^{2})|=|2x-1|,$$
so
$$R_{r}=\frac{3-|2x-1|}{2y}.$$
For fixed $x$, this decreases as $y$ increases, so the minimum occurs on the boundary $y=\sqrt{1-x^{2}}$ (for $x\ge 1/2$; the case $x\le 1/2$ is symmetric). There $|2x-1|=2x-1$ and
$$g(x)=\frac{3-(2x-1)}{2\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}=\frac{2-x}{\sqrt{1-x^{2}}},\qquad x\in[\tfrac12,1).$$
Differentiating,
$$g'(x)=\frac{2x-1}{(1-x^{2})^{3/2}}>0\quad\text{for }x>\tfrac12,$$
so $g$ is strictly increasing on $[\tfrac12,1)$ and attains its minimum at $x=\tfrac12$:
$$g\!\left(\tfrac12\right)=\frac{2-\tfrac12}{\sqrt{1-\tfrac14}}=\frac{3/2}{\sqrt{3}/2}=\sqrt{3}.$$
By symmetry the same value is obtained from $x\le 1/2$. The minimizer is $x=\tfrac12,\ y=\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$, i.e. the **equilateral triangle**, and
$$\inf_{T}\max_{c_{0}} R_{c_{0}}=\sqrt{3}.$$

**Conclusion.** The property holds for every triangle exactly when $\tan\alpha\le\sqrt{3}$, i.e. $\alpha\le \pi/3$. The equilateral triangle shows the bound is sharp: with $|u|=0$, $v=\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}c_{0}$, condition $(\ast)$ reads $\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}c_{0}\tan\alpha\le \tfrac32 c_{0}$, i.e. $\tan\alpha\le\sqrt3$. Therefore the least upper bound is

$$\boxed{\alpha=\dfrac{\pi}{3}}.$$

